Hi! I am a fairly novice TMB user and currently I have rigged up a fairly complex mixed-effects model. I have consulted the CRAN documentation in search of potential ways to speed things up. Under the documentation for MakeADFun() it suggests that compiling with the "TMBad" framework rather than the "CppAD" framework, and setting supernodal = TRUE when calling compile(), can speed things up in some circumstances. I have been using the CppAD framework by default up to this point. When I tried to compile my function with framework = "TMBad" and supernodal = TRUE, it appears to fail. When I set supernodal = FALSE, and keep framework = "TMBad", it compiles fine. For reproducibility purposes, I was able to replicate this issue with the spatial.cpp <https://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/spatial_8cpp-example.html> file available on the Github documentation. Once again, works with supernodal = FALSE but not with supernodal = TRUE. The error messages are quite long and not super intuitive to my eyes; here is a screenshot of some of the messages in case you are interested:
I checked the documentation for TMB::compile() and it seems like setting supernodal = TRUE calls on a different library to do some of the sparse matrix calculations. I imagine the problem is that I don't have the proper version of this library installed, and I'm not exactly sure how to get it installed / how to check whether I have the proper version installed (I use R a lot but don't use C++ very much aside from TMB, and I think it is a C++ library that is being used).

I am running TMB 1.9.9 on R 4.3.2 with a Windows 11 x64 computer. Let me know if there's any additional information I could provide which may be helpful.

Peter

